Independent research complete. FLARE's 60.8% UWM/RoboCasa result is verified (Table 1, UWM trained 400k steps on 7,200 trajectories vs. 80k for other methods). No direct competing paper found for LOC+Value-MLP on WAM action latents specifically. Now issuing final verdict.

```json
{
  "verdict": "fail",
  "scores": {
    "novelty": {
      "score": 3,
      "rationale": "LOC is KNN/GMM density estimation applied to a new representation space (WAM z_a); Value MLP is a 3-layer binary classifier. The novelty rests entirely on the empirical hypothesis that WAM joint pretraining creates more separable latent geometry — a hypothesis with no prior empirical support and that may not hold in action-only inference mode (video tokens at full noise). The three-tier hierarchy is an organizational contribution, not an algorithmic one. After 10 rounds, the method is inherently incremental: off-the-shelf density estimation + off-the-shelf MLP applied to WAM latents."
    },
    "feasibility": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "FLARE's 60.8% figure independently verified as fine-tuned UWM result; frozen UWM baseline at 10-25% is plausible. Budget at 185h H100 is within the 200h constraint. UWM is publicly available. The LOC fallback to Value MLP is correctly gated. Timeline is tight (19 days) but Step 0a validation is correctly first-priority."
    },
    "clarity": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "Exceptional clarity: H1-H5 hypotheses, mandatory LOC diagnostic gate with explicit AUC < 0.55 null-result commitment, three-tier hierarchy, proactive framing against fine-tuned methods, all key citations now in place through Round 10 (FLARE correctly attributed, LPB/LPS/Cortex-2.0/V-GPS/KDPE all differentiated). The scientific design is rigorous and honest."
    },
    "impact": {
      "score": 3,
      "rationale": "Expected RoboCasa performance of 20-35% after ≥10pp improvement over a 10-25% frozen baseline is severely weak against SOTA (FLARE 70.1%, Cosmos Policy 67.1%, World2Act ~66%). The zero-demonstration framing is defensible but narrow. If LOC fails (genuinely uncertain given action-only mode produces z_a from purely noise-conditioned video tokens), the paper reduces to a 3-layer MLP result — insufficient for CoRL's competitive WAM area."
    }
  },
  "issues": [
    "FUNDAMENTAL — Methods are too simple for CoRL 2026's competitive WAM area after 10 rounds of revision. LOC (KNN/GMM nonconformity scoring on success demo latents) and Value MLP (3-layer binary MLP) are off-the-shelf techniques applied to a new representation space. There is no new algorithmic insight, training objective, or architectural contribution. In the hottest CoRL area, competing WAM submissions will have architectural novelty, real-robot experiments, and strong absolute numbers. The 3.5/5.0 average score reflects a quality ceiling the approach cannot break.",
    "FUNDAMENTAL — Expected absolute RoboCasa performance (20-35%) is untenable for CoRL 2026 in the WAM hotspot. Even with honest zero-demonstration framing, reviewers will compare against fine-tuned upper bounds (70.1%, 67.1%, ~66%) and question why the method is being submitted to CoRL rather than a workshop. The authors' proactive framing cannot overcome a 35-50pp gap against contemporary published results on the same benchmark.",
    "FUNDAMENTAL — The central LOC hypothesis is scientifically unproven and fragile. In action-only inference (video timestep = T), video tokens contribute zero inference-time conditioning — z_a is generated from a frozen model with purely noised video context. Whether joint pretraining creates quality-discriminative geometry that persists under this inference mode is genuinely uncertain. The AUC < 0.55 fallback is honest but reveals that the paper's primary novel contribution (LOC) may not exist empirically. If LOC fails, the submission is 'a 3-layer MLP trained on WAM action latents' — a weak standalone CoRL contribution.",
    "STRUCTURAL — After 10 rounds of revision the idea has reached its ceiling. The revision process has correctly identified and integrated all relevant prior work (V-GPS, KDPE, LPB, LPS, Cortex-2.0, FLARE, WAV, Being-H0.7, MG-Select, RoVer), correctly bounded the budget, and correctly framed the scientific contribution. But no amount of citation work or framing can raise a KNN/GMM + 3-layer MLP paper to CoRL 2026 quality in the most competitive area of the venue. The methodology is too thin."
  ],
  "feedback": "This idea is scientifically honest and well-organized but has hit a quality ceiling that cannot be resolved through further revision. The core issue is that LOC + Value MLP are inherently too simple (KNN/GMM + 3-layer MLP) to generate CoRL-level novelty in 2026's WAM hotspot, and expected absolute performance (20-35% RoboCasa) is too weak to overcome reviewer skepticism regardless of framing.\n\nTwo paths forward: (1) Run Step 0a and the LOC diagnostic FIRST. If LOC achieves AUC > 0.70 with substantial variance explained, this is surprising enough to be CoRL-worthy — a genuinely high AUC would be the empirical story itself, not the method. But this requires doing the experiments before deciding to submit, not submitting on the hypothesis. (2) Target ICLR 2027 (deadline ~Oct 2026) with a complete result set: LOC diagnostic (positive or negative), Value MLP ablation, latent geometry visualization (t-SNE of success/failure z_a clusters), and ideally DreamZero cross-WAM validation. A complete empirical paper with definitive answers on the Tier 3 separability hypothesis has a realistic chance at ICLR 2027 — but submitting the hypothesis to CoRL 2026 without results risks a rejection that wastes the 20-day and 185 H100-hour investment.",
  "reason": "Average score 3.5/5.0 after 10 rounds — methods are inherently too simple (KNN/GMM + 3-layer MLP) for CoRL 2026's competitive WAM area, expected absolute performance (20-35% RoboCasa) is untenable, and the central LOC hypothesis is unproven with genuine probability of empirical failure."
}
